WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.840
Alright, so we've talked about Git and GitHub, but how do you actually get things done on GitHub?

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:09.460
How do you go about creating a project and introducing change?

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:15.740
This is the GitHub workflow. There are five basic steps that will walk you through how to introduce change.

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:18.400
So, let's say you're working on a project.

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:21.440
That project would be stored in a GitHub repository

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:25.460
and all of the code that was in production is on a branch called 'master.'

00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:29.880
Now that's important. We don't ever want to make changes directly on to 'master.'

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:33.440
So the first thing we do is create another branch.

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:38.600
When you create a branch it's creating what feels like an exact replica of 'master.'

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:43.420
So you have a safe sandbox to make changes without affecting anything that's in production.

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:46.960
After you create your branch, you make some commits.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:51.380
Now commits are sort of like making changes.

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:59.040
Whenever you make a set of changes you make a commit and it stores all of those changes by taking a snapshot of your repository

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:00.800
Your project at that point in time.

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:03.160
So, if this circle is a commit

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:08.760
You could make one tiny change or you could make a few - Git lets you choose.

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:13.540
Once you've made some commits to introduce your changes you open up a Pull Request on GitHub.com

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:19.440
In this Pull Request you're comparing the branch you made with another branch, like 'master.'

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:26.040
It gives you an opportunity to look at the differences between those two branches, and show other people the differences as well.

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:30.860
Once you do that, and they see the changes, then you can collaborate on them.

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:37.960
They might give you a 'thumbs-up', and say it looks great, or they might have some critiques and suggestions - and that's good, that's the point, right?

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:45.320
So you've opened the Pull Request, you've gotten some feedback, maybe even made some more changes and made some more commits on that branch

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:49.480
Once you've reached a consensus that it's in a good place, you merge.

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:59.500
When you merge, you're taking the branch you created and the commits on that branch and putting them back into 'master' so all of those snapshots are in the production branch.

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:03.880
So, just remember the five basic steps for the GitHub workflow

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:05.440
Create a branch

00:02:05.660 --> 00:02:06.860
Make some commits

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:08.720
Open up a Pull Request

00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:12.160
Collaborate and merge your branch

